    "https://mssu.hrmdirect.com/employment/job-opening.php?req=3827664&req_loc=1490771&&#job": {
      "title": "Facilities Maintenance Specialist II",
      "description": "Facilities Maintenance Specialist II - Careers At Missouri Southern State University Career Opportunities with Missouri Southern State University A great place to work. Careers At Missouri Southern State University Share with friends or Subscribe! Current job opportunities are posted here as they become available. Subscribe to our RSS feeds to receive instant updates as new positions become available. Back To Openings Facilities Maintenance Specialist II Department: Facility Services Location: Joplin, MO START YOUR APPLICATION SUMMARY Facilities Maintenance Specialist II performs a variety of semi-skilled and skilled tasks in all aspects of facility maintenance for all campus buildings and performs the related duties as assigned. At this level, incumbents perform the full range of tasks common to the classification series under less supervision while exercising discretion and independent judgment within established guidelines. Coordinates with other facility technicians in the performance duties and knowledge of minor carpentry, electrical/motors, plumbing/pumps and HVAC systems including chillers and boilers with routine preventive/corrective maintenance functions and projects. Has experience with computers and software usages. This is a day-to-day on-site position, with no remote site capabilities. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES i nclude the following.  Other duties may be assigned. Performs various maintenance functions and capable repairs in carpentry, plumbing, heating and air conditioning, minor painting, and minor electrical repair. Completes assigned work orders and submits a daily task repair list of completed items utilizing the current software. Inspects air conditioning and heating systems to ensure efficient operation; diagnoses electrical and mechanical malfunctions and makes necessary repairs including pumps, motors, belts and pulleys. Including minor preventive duties of lubrication and replacement of belts and air filters. Provides carpentry work associated with all campus buildings including residential life complexes in minor repairs of; drywall, plaster, roofing, doors, ceiling grid/tile and floor surfaces; repairing windows, store front trim, minor painting, staining building fixtures. Installation and assembly of furniture and appliances. Troubleshoots and repairs plumbing systems water fountains, toilets, faucets, showers and drains in campus facilities; replaces plumbing pipes, flush valves and maintains sanitary sewer lines and systems. Performs minor electrical and low voltage repairs, including lighting, light fixtures, minor control modules, VFD’s, relays and contactors. Monitors campus software for control systems of HVAC/Key logs including site inspections assigned for equipment monitoring makes the necessary decisions in equipment settings for thermostats, building valves for proper cooling and heating. Maintains the associated records as needed. For work that cannot be performed in-house assists in contacting, scheduling and coordinating contracted maintenance services, such as HVAC, plumbing and electrical work. May include contracted roofers, door & glass installers and other unique contractor repairs. Has oversight for their work and monitoring the project till completion, verifying work invoices. Assists in campus events setup, including bleachers, chairs, tables and staging as needed or as stand in for any event maintenance needs. This may include commencements, holidays or other scheduled weekend events. (Example working Fourth of July) This position requires and is expected to be on-call, working overtime, weekends, evenings, and holidays to accommodate the campus needs, it may require being a shift that is rotational with other like positions. In addition, responding if called for campus emergencies including snow and ice events. Must have consistent and regular attendance for position requirements. DIRECT SKILLS & ABILITIES NEEDED The use of related hand and power tools. Including Carpentry, HVAC, Electrical and Plumbing. Skills in the refrigeration field. This should include installations, diagnosing and repair of small appliances up to industrial equipment.  Have experience and knowledge in hydronics and other applications of heating and chiller systems. This includes installation, trouble shooting and repair of gas fired furnaces, low-pressure steam boilers, chillers, pumping systems and air handling equipment. Skills in electrical trade include diagnosing o f industrial equipment, fixtures and controls. Utilizing the proper tools and safety methods. Be able to read designed schematics and install electrical fixtures and equipment involved in various construction projects. Have a working understanding of DDC automated controls, pneumatic controls and other control systems.  Should have a general knowledge of computers operations (prefer Microsoft Word and Excel). Work independently in the absence of direct supervision. Understand and carry out oral and written instructions. Communicate clearly and concisely, both orally and in writing. Maintain physical condition appropriate to the performance of assigned duties and responsibilities including mental capacity, which allows the capability of making sound decisions and demonstrating intellectual capabilities. .Capability in establishing and maintaining positive cooperative working relationships with those contacted in the course of work. QUALIFICATIONS To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily.  The requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required.  Reasonable accommodation may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Capabilities in the reading of building plans and blueprints, electrical schematics, and piping system diagrams. Past hands-on experience record reflecting corresponding work in the related field. PRIOR EDUCATION and/or EXPERIENCE. Higher Educational Degree or recognized trade school is preferred; high school graduate or GED required. A minimum of four years verified experience in general or trades related maintenance or certification from a recognized trade school and two years of acceptable experience or any journeyman certificate considered. LANGUAGE SKILLS. Ability to read/speak English preferred, understand and interpret procedure manuals, work orders, equipment operating instructions, blueprints, specifications and general correspondence (ie: memos, letters, etc.).  Ability to effectively communicate orally with staff, students, administration and visitors.  Ability to read and accurately complete maintenance requests, time sheets, supply requests, etc. MATHEMATICAL SKILLS. Ability to calculate figures and amounts or proportions in square footage. Ability to apply concepts of basic math for basic calculations. REASONING ABILITY. Ability to solve practical problems and deal with a variety of variables in situations where only limited standardization exists.  Ability to interpret a variety of instructions furnished in written, oral, diagram or schedule form. CERTIFICATES, LICENSES, REGISTRATIONS. Requires a minimum Type 2 Refrigerant Transition and Recovery Certification. Universal Certificate preferred. Must be able to take associated testing for acceptable certification for HVAC within six months of start date. Must have valid Driver’s License and ability to meet the University’s insurance requirements for driving university vehicles. PHYSICAL DEMANDS. The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to stand; use hands to finger, handle or feel including the use of a computer keyboard; reach with hands and arms; work on their feet for several hours at a time; and bend, stoop, climb ladder/stairs.  The employee is frequently required to lift and/or move up to 65 lbs., may require working overhead, using a ladder or scaffolding.  The employee is occasionally required to work at certain heights and be able to climb extension ladders and work while balancing, stooping, kneeling, crouching, or moving within confined areas including equipment, crawl and attic spaces; and talk or hear including the use of a telephone and 2-way radio.  Specific vision abilities required by this job include close vision, distance vision, color vision, peripheral vision, depth perception, and ability to adjust focus. WORK ENVIRONMENT. The work environment characteristics described here are representative of those an employee encounters while performing the essential functions of this job.  Reasonable accommodation may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Typically, the employee works indoors, in classroom, business office and athletic facility environments with a moderate noise level.  Frequently the employee is required to work in the maintenance shop or mechanical rooms with loud power equipment.  Occasionally the employee will be required to work outside the building during inclement weather for construction, repair and maintenance functions START YOUR APPLICATION Visit Our Home Page © 2026 Missouri Southern State University Applicant Tracking System Powered by"
    },
    "https://mssu.hrmdirect.com/employment/job-opening.php?req=3808575&req_loc=1463654&&#job": {
      "title": "Scholarship and Outreach Counselor Financial Aid",
      "description": "Scholarship and Outreach Counselor  Financial Aid - Careers At Missouri Southern State University Career Opportunities with Missouri Southern State University A great place to work. Careers At Missouri Southern State University Share with friends or Subscribe! Current job opportunities are posted here as they become available. Subscribe to our RSS feeds to receive instant updates as new positions become available. Back To Openings Scholarship and Outreach Counselor - Financial Aid Department: Financial Aid Office Location: Joplin, MO START YOUR APPLICATION General Statement of Job The Scholarship and Outreach Counselor administers University scholarship programs, including Institutional, Foundation, departmental, performance-based, athletic, outside, international student, and other scholarship opportunities administered by the University. This individual works closely with students, families, coaches, campus departments, the MSSU Foundation, and other University personnel to ensure timely, accurate, and consistent administration of scholarship funds. The position also performs Financial Aid Counselor duties, provides financial aid and scholarship guidance to prospective, new, and continuing students, and participates in outreach and educational activities on behalf of the University. In addition, this position supports federal consumer information compliance and other related Student Financial Support Services responsibilities. Specific Duties and Responsibilities Essential Duties: Serves as the primary University resource for scholarship programs, policies, eligibility requirements, application procedures, renewal requirements and award administration for prospective students, current students, families, high school counselors, coaches, campus departments and other internal and external constituents. Administers Institutional, Foundation, departmental, performance-based, athletic, outside, international student, and other scholarship programs administered by the University. Identifies student eligibility for Institutional and Foundation scholarships through University systems, scholarship applications, student records, and other applicable documentation. Reviews and communicates scholarship awards to prospective and continuing students and manages acceptance and processing of awards. Coordinates Foundation scholarship application and award process, including reviewing applications for completeness and eligibility, preparing applicant information for committee review, documenting award decisions, processing approved awards, and coordinating renewals and related communication in collaboration with the MSSU Foundation. Coordinates and supports the University Scholarship Committee including preparation of materials, interpretation of scholarship policies and criteria, documentation of decisions, and recommendations regarding scholarship selection criteria policies and procedures. Coordinates the awarding, posting, adjustment, renewal, cancellation, and monitoring of Institutional, Foundation, departmental, performance-based, athletic, outside and other scholarships within University systems and student accounts Works closely with Athletics staff and coaches to administer athletic scholarships, including establishment, adjustment, renewal, cancellation, and monitoring of awards. Ensures athletic scholarship information is accurately maintained in University systems and coordinates with appropriate personnel to resolve discrepancies and support compliance with applicable University, financial aid, and athletic requirements. Coordinates departmental and performance-based scholarship awards with campus departments to ensure timely awarding, accurate records, appropriate use of funds, and adherence to established scholarship criteria and requirements. Works closely with International Admissions to process required documentation and administer scholarships for eligible international students. Processes third-party and outside scholarships, including receipt, review, posting, and resolution of scholarship funds, and coordinates with appropriate Student Financial Support Services personnel to ensure funds are accurately applied to student accounts. Evaluates scholarship recipients’ academic and enrollment status each term to determine continued eligibility and renewal. Communicates eligibility decisions to affected students and provides information regarding renewal requirements, improvement expectations, reinstatement criteria, and appeal processes as appropriate. Assists students with scholarship appeals and supporting documentation and prepares information for committee review. Monitors scholarship disbursements and performs reconciliation activities for Institutional, Foundation, athletic, departmental, performance-based, and other scholarship programs. Identifies and resolves discrepancies and prepares scholarship-related reports for internal and external use, including year-end reporting. Reviews scholarship policies, procedures, forms, communications and processes on an ongoing basis and recommends updates and improvements. Maintains accurate and current scholarship information on the University websites and other student-facing resources, including information regarding University and outside scholarship opportunities. Performs Financial Aid Counselor duties for prospective, new, and continuing students regarding federal, state, institutional, and private financial aid programs; application requirements; eligibility; enrollment requirements; satisfactory academic progress; deadlines; processing requirements; and other financial aid policies and procedures. Reviews student financial aid records and documentation, assists students in resolving financial aid issues, and provides guidance regarding required forms, missing information, eligibility concerns, award adjustments, and next steps. Counsels students and families regarding FAFSA, financial aid eligibility, cost of attendance, aid offers, grants, scholarships, loans, satisfactory academic progress, and other financial aid requirements and responsibilities. Assists students with financial aid appeals and other student-specific review as assigned, including explaining documentation requirements, timelines and available options. Provides financial aid and scholarship guidance to students participating in Study Abroad and other programs requiring individualized financial aid review. Plans, coordinates and participates in financial aid and scholarship outreach and educational activities, including FAFSA completion events, Lion Pride Visit Days, high school counselor outreach, recruitment activities, and presentations for prospective students and families, campus groups, and community organizations.  Some evening and weekend or off-campus participation may be required. Communicates with public and private high school personnel regarding University scholarship and financial aid opportunities, application processes, and related requirements and provides scholarship materials and presentation information as appropriate. Supports federal consumer information compliance by assisting with the review, monitoring, and maintenance of required financial aid and scholarship disclosures and information. Maintains complete and accurate records necessary for internal and external reporting requirements. and ensures student information and documentation are maintained appropriately within Banner, electronic document systems, and other University records. Monitors payment of scholarship disbursements and manages reconciliation activities for Institutional and Foundation scholarships. Provides scholarship reports for internal or external entities and year-end reporting. Assists or develops and reviews scholarship and financial aid communications, forms, publications, presentations and web content to ensure information is accurate, current, and understandable. Contributes to the development and implementation of Student Financial Support Services policies, procedures and service practices. Cross-trains in other Student Financial Support Services functions and performs Financial Aid Counselor and other office duties as need to support students and office operations. Works collaboratively with other University departments to ensure timely, accurate, and coordinated delivery of student financial services. Performs other related duties as assigned. Education, Experience, and Licenses Bachelor's degree from an accredited four-year college or university is required. One or two years related experience and/or training preferred; or an equivalent combination of education and experience preferred. Demonstrated knowledge of financial aid preferred. Experience in handling multiple tasks and customer service orientation with a commitment to higher education and working with students is a plus. Willingness to travel occasionally for presentations, meetings, and training workshops, as required. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities To perform this job successfully, an individual must be able to perform each essential duty satisfactorily. The requirements listed below are representative of the knowledge, skill, and/or ability required. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Excellent organizational, verbal and written communication skills. Ability to work collaboratively as part of a team environment. Ability to use highly technical computer applications, such as Banner, Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, Access, PowerPoint), and other web-based applications to perform office duties. Ability to maintain highest level of confidentiality in accordance with the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA). Physical Demands/Work Environment The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to talk or hear including the use of the telephone. The employee frequently is required to stand. The employee is occasionally required to walk; sit; use hands to finger, handle, or feel including the use of a computer keyboard; reach with hands and arms; and stoop, kneel, or crouch. The employee must frequently lift and/or move up to 50 pounds. Specific vision abilities required by this job include close vision, distance vision, color vision, peripheral vision, depth perception, and ability to adjust focus. The noise level in the work environment is usually moderate. NOTICE : The above job profile does not include all essential and nonessential duties of this job. All employees with disabilities are encouraged to contact Human Resources to review and discuss the essential and nonessential functions of the job. An employee with a disability can evaluate the job in greater detail to determine if she/he can safely perform the essential function of this job with or without reasonable accommodation. START YOUR APPLICATION Visit Our Home Page © 2026 Missouri Southern State University Applicant Tracking System Powered by"
    },
    "https://mssu.hrmdirect.com/employment/job-opening.php?req=3780616&req_loc=1420454&&#job": {
